{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Automate the cleanup of images by using lifecycle policies in Amazon ECR","context":"### Lifecycle policy evaluation rules\n\nThe lifecycle policy evaluator is responsible for parsing the plaintext JSON of\nthe lifecycle policy, evaluating all rules, and then applying those rules based on\nrule priority to the images in the repository. The following explains the logic of\nthe lifecycle policy evaluator in more detail. For examples, see Examples of lifecycle policies in Amazon ECR.\n\n* When reference artifacts are present in a repository, Amazon ECR lifecycle\n policies automatically expire or archive those artifacts within 24 hours of the\n deletion or archival of the subject image.\n* All rules are evaluated at the same time, regardless of rule priority.\n After all rules are evaluated, they are then applied based on rule\n priority.\n* An image is expired or archived by exactly one or zero rules.\n* An image that matches the tagging requirements of a rule cannot be expired\n or archived by a rule with a lower priority.\n* Rules can never mark images that are marked by higher priority rules, but\n can still identify them as if they haven't been expired or archived.\n* The set of all rules selecting a specific storage class must contain a unique set of prefixes.\n* Only one rule selecting a specific storage class is allowed to select untagged images.\n* If an image is referenced by a manifest list, it cannot be expired or archived without\n the manifest list being deleted or archived first.\n* Expiration is always ordered by `pushed_at_time` or `transitioned_at_time` and always expires older images before newer ones. If an image was archived and then restored at any point in the past, the image's `last_activated_at` is used instead of `pushed_at_time`.\n* A lifecycle policy rule may specify either `tagPatternList` or\n `tagPrefixList`, but not both. However, a lifecycle policy\n may contain multiple rules where different rules use both pattern and prefix\n lists. An image is successfully matched if all of the tags in the\n `tagPatternList` or `tagPrefixList` value are\n matched against any of the image's tags.\n* The `tagPatternList` or `tagPrefixList` parameters\n may only used if the `tagStatus` is `tagged`.\n* When using `tagPatternList`, an image is successfully matched\n if it matches the wildcard filter. For example, if a filter of\n `prod*` is applied, it would\n\n*[truncated — additional content omitted]*","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/LifecyclePolicies.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"How do I check my Amazon ECR lifecycle policy events?","context":"Short Description\n-----------------\n\nA [lifecycle policy](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/LifecyclePolicies.html) allows you to specify the lifecycle management of images in your Amazon ECR repository. This lets you automate the clean-up of your container images. For example, the policy can instruct Amazon ECR to expire images based on age or count. After you create a lifecycle policy, the affected images expire within 24 hours.\n\nTo check if your lifecycle policy deleted a specific image, use AWS CloudTrail.\n\nResolution\n----------\n\n1. Open the [CloudTrail Console](https://console.aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/).\n2. In the navigation pane, choose **Event history**.\n3. For **Lookup attributes**, choose **Event Name** as the filter. Then, enter **PolicyExecutionEvent** as the event name.\n4. Select the time range of the event that want to search for. Then, choose **Apply**.\n5. CloudTrail shows events, similar to the following example:\n ```plaintext\n {\n \"eventVersion\": \"1.05\",\n \"userIdentity\": {\n \"accountId\": \"012345678901_2\",\n \"invokedBy\": \"AWS Internal\"\n },\n \"eventTime\": \"2020-03-12T20:22:12Z\",\n \"eventSource\": \"ecr.amazonaws.com\",\n \"eventName\": \"PolicyExecutionEvent\",\n \"awsRegion\": \"us-west-2\",\n \"sourceIPAddress\": \"AWS Internal\",\n \"userAgent\": \"AWS Internal\",\n \"requestParameters\": null,\n \"responseElements\": null,\n \"eventID\": \"9354dd7f-9aac-4e9d-956d-12561a4923aa\",\n \"readOnly\": true,\n \"resources\": [{\n \"ARN\": \"[redacted ARN]\",\n \"accountId\": \"012345678901_2\",\n \"type\": \"AWS::ECR::Repository\"\n }],\n \"eventType\": \"AwsServiceEvent\",\n \"recipientAccountId\": \"012345678901_2\",\n \"serviceEventDetails\": {\n \"repositoryName\": \"testrepo\",\n \"lifecycleEventPolicy\": {\n \"lifecycleEventRules\": [{\n \"rulePriority\": 1,\n \"description\": \"remove all images >\n\n*[truncated — additional content omitted]*","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/ecr-lifecycle-policy-events"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Examples of lifecycle policies in Amazon ECR","context":"### Example B\n\nThe following example illustrates that tags are not exclusive.\n\nRepository contents:\n\n* Image A, Taglist: [\"alpha-1\", \"beta-1\", \"gamma-1\"], Pushed: 10 days\n ago\n* Image B, Taglist: [\"alpha-2\", \"beta-2\"], Pushed: 9 days ago\n* Image C, Taglist: [\"alpha-3\", \"beta-3\", \"gamma-2\"], Pushed: 8 days\n ago\n\n```\n{\n \"rules\": [\n {\n \"rulePriority\": 1,\n \"description\": \"Rule 1\",\n \"selection\": {\n \"tagStatus\": \"tagged\",\n \"tagPatternList\": [\"alpha*\", \"beta*\"],\n \"countType\": \"imageCountMoreThan\",\n \"countNumber\": 1\n },\n \"action\": {\n \"type\": \"expire\"\n }\n }\n ]\n}\n```\n\nThe logic of this lifecycle policy would be:\n\n* Rule 1 identifies images tagged with prefix `alpha` and\n `beta`. It sees all images. It should mark images,\n starting with the oldest, until there is one or fewer images remaining\n that match. It marks image A and B for expiration.\n* Result: Images A and B are expired","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/lifecycle_policy_examples.html"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon ECR introduces archive storage class for rarely accessed container images - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced a new archive storage class to help reduce storage costs for infrequently accessed container images. This new feature allows users to meet compliance and retention requirements while optimizing storage expenses. ECR's lifecycle policies now support archiving images based on last pull time, enabling automatic archiving based on usage patterns. Users can also archive images individually using the ECR Console or API. Archived images do not count towards the image per repository limit and are inaccessible for pulls but can be easily restored within 20 minutes via the ECR Console, CLI, or API. All archival and restore operations are logged through CloudTrail for auditability. The new ECR archive storage class is available in all AWS Commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For pricing details, visit the AWS pricing page. To learn more, consult the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-ecr-archive-storage-class-container-images/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon ECR now supports 25 replication rules per registry - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) has increased the maximum number of replication rules per registry from 10 to 25. This update allows customers with complex multi-region or multi-account architectures to define up to 25 replication rules per registry, enabling more precise replication strategies for distributing images across many regions for low-latency pulls or replicating to multiple production and staging accounts. This service limit increase is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon ECR is supported","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/amazon-ecr-increased-replication-rules-limit/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-resilience-lifecycle","skill_description":"Guides the end-to-end AWS resilience lifecycle integrating Resilience Hub v2, Fault Injection Service, and Application Recovery Controller. Covers the Define → Test → Operate workflow: from policy creation through failure mode assessment, to FIS experiment validation, to ARC operational controls. Applicable when the user wants a complete resilience strategy, needs to connect findings to experiments to controls, or is planning a resilience program. Also applicable for the meta question of whether marking NGRH findings as resolved is enough, whether they are \"done\" after resolving findings, or how to validate findings before resolving them. Not applicable for resolving or remediating a specific individual finding (see resilience-hub-failure-mode-assessment), or when a single service is explicitly named (e.g. \"what FIS experiment should I run\").\n\n\nServices: resiliencehub, fis, route53-recovery-control-config, arc-zonal-shift\nTasks: configure, test, audit\nPersona: architect, sre, devops\nWorkload: resilience\n*","skill_name":"aws-resilience-lifecycle"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"EC2 Image Builder enhances lifecycle policies with wildcard support and simplified IAM - AWS","context":"EC2 Image Builder, a service offered by AWS for automating the creation, distribution, and management of customized Amazon Machine Images, has introduced two new features to simplify image management. Firstly, wildcard patterns are now supported in lifecycle policies, allowing users to manage images from multiple recipes within a single policy. This feature automatically applies lifecycle policies to all matching recipes, including new ones created in the future. Secondly, IAM role creation for lifecycle management has been simplified, with the console now automatically populating the required default permissions when creating a new role. Previously, users had to create separate lifecycle policies for each new recipe or manually configure permissions, making it difficult to manage image lifecycles at scale. These enhancements reduce setup time and potential configuration errors, enabling users to manage their image lifecycle more efficiently. Lifecycle Policies are available in all commercial AWS regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/ec2-image-builder-lifecycle-enhancements/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Amazon ECR now supports creating repositories on push - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced a new feature that allows automatic repository creation upon image push. This simplifies container workflows by eliminating the need for customers to pre-create repositories before pushing container images. When an image is pushed, ECR will create a repository based on defined repository creation template settings. This capability is available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For more information on repository creation templates and other ECR features, visit the product page and user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/amazon-ecr-creating-repositories-on-push/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify ECR lifecycle policy rule evaluation order semantics in docs."}
